.TH mnnn-net 1  "mnnn-net 2.5.1" 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
mnnn\-net \- The network: run a node, exchange keys with other nodes, share data with them, search across them and entrust them with dark shards. Off and sharing nothing until you ask
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmnnn net\fR [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-theme\-mode\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
The network: run a node, exchange keys with other nodes, share data with them, search across them and entrust them with dark shards. Off and sharing nothing until you ask
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Muninn home directory (default ~/.muninn)
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_HOME\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Database file (default <home>/storage/muninn.database)
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR
Never prompt; missing values are errors
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_NON_INTERACTIVE\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
More output (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing but errors and requested data
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_QUIET\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to every confirmation
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_YES\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\-mode\fR \fI<THEME_MODE>\fR
Override the theme mode for this run
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
light
.IP \(bu 2
dark
.IP \(bu 2
system
.RE
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBMUNINN_THEME_MODE\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH NOTES
.PP
The node starts only when you run `mnnn net start` (or Network ▸ Start node in the TUI); the
other commands bring up a short\-lived node with the same identity when they need to talk.
Nothing is shared until you `share` it, and peers only see it after a completed key exchange.
.PP
Key exchange: one side makes an offer (a token, or sent straight to a peer with \-\-peer); the
other accepts it and both get the same key and a short code. Compare the codes out of band and
`verify`. Revoking erases the key on both sides (the record stays in the history).
.PP
Examples:
  mnnn net start \-\-for 3600
  mnnn net exchange create \-\-label ben \-\-peer /ip4/192.0.2.7/tcp/4741/p2p/12D3Koo…
  mnnn net exchange accept 3f2a                 # an incoming offer (or paste a token)
  mnnn net exchange verify ben 12345\-67890
  mnnn net share journal "field notes" \-\-with ben
  mnnn net search \-\-journals \-\-query comet
  mnnn net fetch ben "field notes" \-\-import
  mnnn net shard entrust \-\-to ben \-\-to cleo \-\-to dev \-\-threshold 2
  mnnn net shard restore \-\-backup net\-backup.age \-\-recover
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
mnnn\-net\-start(1)
Run this node in the foreground until Ctrl\-C (or \-\-for SECS, or `mnnn net stop`)
.TP
mnnn\-net\-stop(1)
Ask a node started with `mnnn net start` to stop
.TP
mnnn\-net\-status(1)
This node: identity, addresses, connections, NAT, relays
.TP
mnnn\-net\-peers(1)
Peers seen or known
.TP
mnnn\-net\-connect(1)
Say hello to a node (`…/p2p/ID`, a peer id or a known peer) and remember it
.TP
mnnn\-net\-forget(1)
Forget a known peer (exchanges with it are kept)
.TP
mnnn\-net\-relay(1)
Relays
.TP
mnnn\-net\-config(1)
Show or change network settings
.TP
mnnn\-net\-exchange(1)
Key exchanges with other nodes
.TP
mnnn\-net\-share(1)
Share an item: model, search, journal, project, report or code
.TP
mnnn\-net\-unshare(1)
Stop sharing an item (everywhere, or with \-\-with)
.TP
mnnn\-net\-shared(1)
What this node shares, and with whom
.TP
mnnn\-net\-list(1)
What a peer shares with us (all peers with an exchange when none is given)
.TP
mnnn\-net\-fetch(1)
Fetch an item a peer shares
.TP
mnnn\-net\-search(1)
Search here and on every peer that shares with you; results say where they came from
.TP
mnnn\-net\-shard(1)
Dark shards: the master key split among peers you exchanged keys with
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
